G Suite is Cloud Identity?

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.460
OK, good.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.160
How many of you have heard
of the term BeyondCorp.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.540
OK, great.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.210
And how many of you think
that device management

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.320
is a part of Cloud Identity?

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.380
OK, great.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:37.880
OK.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.760
I hope you'll find the
session today interesting.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.840
So we'll see.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.580
So we'll cover the following
topics this afternoon--

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.370
Woodstock with an overview
of Cloud Identity.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.050
We'll go over the
various features,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.830
talk about the roadmap, go
over some exciting demos.

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.860
Colin will talk about
their Cloud Identity

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.380
experience and
journey at Essence

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.610
and we'll tie all this together.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:06.840
And at the end, we will
have some time for Q&amp;A.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.820
So it's pretty amazing to see
how the generation of today

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.220
has got vastly different
expectations from what

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.020
I had growing up.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.710
So technology has come a really
long way in the last 20 years.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.620
There was a time when
my computer at work

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.840
was a lot better than
what I had at home,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.670
and my internet
connectivity at work

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.810
was way better than
what I had at home.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.500
And in fact, I
would come to work,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.080
and I could only access data
within my office boundaries,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.120
because I knew no better.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.830
Today's generation,
on the contrary,

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.060
expects way more flexibility.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.580
They want to be
able to access data

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.130
from anywhere, from any device.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.410
And the workspace
as we know it is

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.320
going through a massive
digital transformation

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.060
just to support their needs.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.630
And you, as IT decision
makers, are at the forefront,

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.250
making all this happen.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.990
And identity and
access management

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:59.403
is front and center
of this revolution.

00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:00.820
So we're living
in a perimeterless

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.210
or an edgeless world, a world
in which the enterprise security

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.990
perimeter is no longer
your office front door.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.960
But instead, it is elastic,
and it's dispersed.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.960
And it's wrapped around
each user and each device.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.120
And everything is
based on context.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.800
And your users are not
just your employees.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.410
But now they are your vendors,
they're your contractors,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.420
they're your customers.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:25.480
And all of these different
people have different needs,

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.800
and they need to access
different information,

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.920
different applications,
from different places,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.960
and from different devices.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.270
So clearly, a different
approach to identity and access

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.610
management is critical
and important.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.670
Existing approaches are very
complex, very time consuming,

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:40.930
and very cumbersome.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.210
They were built for
the on-premise world,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.240
and they are not optimized for
today's cloud-forward world.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.950
So with more users requesting
access for more information

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.650
and from more devices
than ever before,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.020
IT teams struggle with a
complex network of managing

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.480
applications, devices, users.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.690
And shadow IT is also a
huge problem in this space.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:04.250
But therein also
lies our opportunity.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.030
So in 2011, Google came
up with a new model

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.920
of defining access
management, which

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.930
is what we call as BeyondCorp.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.120
It's a new enterprise
security model

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.370
which started as an
internal Google initiative

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.010
to enable every employee
to be able to work

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:22.330
from untrusted networks without
the use of a traditional VPN.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.130
BeyondCorp typically
shifts the access

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.470
controls from that of
the network perimeter

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:27.820
to that of the application.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.820
It's all around each user
and each device, thereby

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.630
allowing employees to work
securely from any location.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:39.160
So Cloud Identity helps bring
the BeyondCorp provision

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.900
to your organization.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.380
It is designed around the core
pillars of being simple, smart,

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:45.550
and secure.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:49.300
So people, devices,
applications, directory,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.930
and extensible APIs are
all first class citizens,

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.990
managed within a unified,
single pane of glass.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:04:00.480
People, devices, and context
is your new security perimeter.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.080
So policies are
defined, and they're

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.660
enforced, to
control access based

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.600
on the user's identity and
the context of that request.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.380
So while Cloud Identity
helps you as an organization

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.510
to move towards
the cloud, we also

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.540
want to make sure we meet the
organization where they are

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.399
in their cloud
journey by supporting

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:22.310
your on-premise identity and
access management systems.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.490
So in the next section,
we'll talk about some

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.650
of the key features
of Cloud Identity,

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.430
and we'll also go
over the roadmap.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.210
But I want you to remember
that Cloud Identity enjoys

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.880
the following key
differentiators--

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.070
first and foremost,
Cloud Identity

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.440
enjoys Google grade security.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.840
You can control access
to SaaS applications.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.330
And for strong,
multi-factor authentication,

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:47.140
protect your users and accounts,
get automatic protections

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.170
against hijacking with
machine learning data

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.870
from Google services.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.600
And above all, we ensure
that everything is simple.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.730
It's simple not just for
the IT administrator,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.730
but also for the end user.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:03.100
And it's about meeting you
where you are in your journey.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.940
And we believe in supporting
your hybrid identity story.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.150
So let's first talk
about the people.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.060
With Cloud Identity, enterprises
can automatically provision

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:16.300
accounts into Google's Cloud
Directory using APIs or using

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.910
our tool called the Google
Cloud Directory Sync.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.190
Accounts can also be
automatically provisioned

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.710
into third party systems
using a feature called

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:24.870
auto provisioning.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.760
And that ensures both
end user as well as

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.560
administrative convenience, and
also has security compliance.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.920
Google protects all
Cloud Identity accounts

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.740
against account takeover,
compromise, or hijacking

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.840
through the use of
machine learning data

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.090
from over eight services
with over one billion

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.440
plus 30-day active users.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.740
So Cloud Identity
offers multiple options

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.860
to protect user accounts
with strong authentication

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:53.110
methods which we call
2-step verification.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.180
That includes push
notifications,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.900
one-time passwords,
password alerts, and so on.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.280
And further, as awareness
of potential vulnerabilities

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.280
associated with telephony
options like SMS or voice codes

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:11.090
has increased, we also provide
admins with the ability--

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.440
we give them the choice--

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.500
to disable these telephony
options like SMS or voice codes

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:20.240
if they so choose or desire as
a 2SV method for their users.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.360
We've also long advocated
the use of security keys

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.970
as the strongest and the
most phishing-resistant

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:29.670
authentication method
for high-value users.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.990
Customers have long told us
that it can be hard to source

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:34.820
and roll out security keys.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.530
So we have the
Titan Security Key,

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.840
which is a FIDO-based
security SKI that

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.810
includes firmware that has been
developed by Google to verify

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:42.860
its integrity.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.990
It's available through
the Google Store,

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.330
but it's rolling out in
other regions very soon.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:54.200
So we'd also like to announce
that you can use your Android

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.010
phone now as a security key.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.040
This is a feature that's
available in beta.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.610
And this offers both strongest
protection and authentication

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:05.150
for your users, while offers
convenience for the users.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.340
Because now, it's a
one-time enrollment process.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.010
There is no additional
hardware or apps required,

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:12.860
and you get that strong
authentication and protection

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:15.650
for your high value users.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.470
Cloud Identity also comes
with the Security Center

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.830
that provides controls from
prevention t detection,

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:22.800
to remediation.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.210
So admins can manage and
improve their security posture

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:27.290
within the organization.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.140
For example, they can get
proactive phishing protection.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.230
They can get actionable security
insights through the dashboard,

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:35.300
for example, phishing risks.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.330
And they can diagnose, triage,
and resolve security incidents

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.430
through the investigation tool.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.070
We're excited to announce
that Cloud Identity will now

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.350
allow integration
with third party HRIS

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:52.910
systems such as BambooHR,
ADP, Ultimate Pro, and Namely,

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:55.460
to enable automated user
lifecycle management.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.220
And this will be generally
available in the summer.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:00.500
And Shivani will now
give a quick peek

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.940
at what this will look like.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:05.710
SHIVANI SINHA: Hello, everyone.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.770
So here is a quick peek
at what this integration

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:10.220
is going to look like.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:12.100
Let's say your
company uses BambooHR

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.290
for storing employee
information,

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:17.800
and now your IP admin wants to
sync this employee information

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.070
from BambooHR to Cloud Identity.

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:23.230
The way they can do
that is by accessing

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:26.860
the external directory
section of the admin console.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.860
Here, you can add your
BambooHR external directory.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:33.070
When you click on Add,
you get to this page

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:36.400
where you can add some brief
information about BambooHR.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.471
As you can see, we need to also
add a service account, Now,

00:08:39.471 --> 00:08:40.929
this service account
is going to be

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:43.960
used to sync the employee
information from BambooHR

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.210
to Cloud Identity.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:50.260
When you click on Add
Directory, a SCIM endpoint URL

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.350
is generated, and now you need
to copy this endpoint URL,

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:55.900
and paste it into
BambooHR setup page.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:59.110
Now, that syncs the employee
information from BambooHR

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.910
to Cloud Identity.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:04.720
Once the sync is setup, any
time you add, modify, or delete

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.870
a user in BambooHR, this
information gets automatically

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.990
updated in Cloud Identity,
and your company no longer

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:13.795
needs to maintain any manual
sync between these two systems.

00:09:19.375 --> 00:09:20.500
VIDYA NAGARAJAN: OK, great.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.240
So now, let's talk
about applications.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.740
So we first spoke
about people, now,

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.400
let's talk about applications.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:27.640
Cloud Identity comes
with a very rich catalog

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.400
of thousands of federated
apps that support SAML as well

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.230
as OIDC standards.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:33.730
In addition, we're
happy to announce

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:36.400
that Cloud Identity
will also support

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.430
password-based single sign-on
into those applications that

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.570
do not support the
federated standards.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.660
This will be generally
available end of the summer.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:46.240
And this was a key
feature that was

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.890
a part of our VDM acquisition.

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:51.740
Also coming soon is
a new end user hub

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.680
where users can quickly
access all of their apps

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:56.930
from one single place.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.030
A key tenet of Cloud
Identity is the ability

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.540
to meet organizations where
they are in their cloud journey.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:04.730
While the adoption of SaaS
apps continues to grow,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:09.230
many businesses still rely on
traditional, legacy on-premise

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.800
LDAP-based applications.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.130
So Secure LDAP in
Cloud Identity allows

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.080
you to use a single
access management

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.450
platform to access
both your SaaS

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.310
as well as your on-premise
LDAP applications.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:22.130
So what this means
is that admins

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:25.040
can manage both the SaaS as
well as your on-premise LDAP

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.590
applications from one
single control plane.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:30.440
And end users can use the same
Cloud Identity credentials

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:32.330
and can log into
both the SaaS as well

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.910
as on-prem applications.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.570
So we have several partners
that are integrating with Secure

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.190
LDAP today, and we're happy
to announce our latest

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.560
entrant to the Cloud, GitLab.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.110
So now, let's see
this in action.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.710
SHIVANI SINHA: Thank you, Vidya.

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:53.000
So let's see a demo
of Secure LDAP.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.630
Now, let's say your company
uses GitLab to write and manage

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:57.380
code.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.810
And now, your admin
wants to configure

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.600
Secure LDAP with GitLab
so your engineering

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.060
team can log in to GitLab
using their Cloud Identity

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.370
credentials.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.290
The way, this is going
to work is the admin

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:12.290
has to first go to the LDAP
section of the Admin console,

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:14.130
as you can see on the screen.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:15.980
Now, you can add
a new LDAP client

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:18.330
by clicking on Add Client.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.830
Let us give our
LDAP client a name.

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:21.792
Let's call it GitLab.

00:11:21.792 --> 00:11:23.500
You can add a description
if you want to,

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:25.650
but for now, I'm
going to skip it.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.210
Let's click on Continue.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:29.430
Now, this takes me to the
access permissions page,

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.820
where I can configure access
permissions for my GitLab LDAP

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.020
client.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:36.780
I can figure out how much
of my directory information

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.860
this client should
be able to access.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:41.280
For example, I can
click on entire domain

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:44.280
if all my employees should be
able to log in and to GitLab,

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:47.510
or I can select a specific OU.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:49.955
Now, if I click
on Edit, I can see

00:11:49.955 --> 00:11:51.330
that I can select
the Engineering

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:55.690
OU, because only my engineers
need to write code in GitLab.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.510
Let's hit Save.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.610
When I scroll down,
I can also select

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:02.040
what user and group
information the client

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.690
should be able to get access to.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:08.680
Let's click on Add, and
click on Engineering,

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:12.200
because the client should only
be able to access Engineering.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.400
Let's allow the client to
read group information.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.140
And now, click on
Add LDAP Client.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:23.050
Now, this should
generate a certificate.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:24.880
You need to now download
this certificate

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.610
and then upload the certificate
to your LDAP client.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:31.720
You can do so for GitLab by
following this public document

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:33.580
that GitLab has published.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:37.280
By reading the instructions in
the Configuring GitLab section,

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.460
you can now upload
your certificate

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:43.680
and set that sync between
GitLab and Cloud Identity.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.590
Once this is done, what
it means for employees

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:51.420
is your engineering team can
now use their Cloud Identity

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.340
credentials to log in to GitLab.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:56.490
And your admin can now use
the same admin console,

00:12:56.490 --> 00:13:00.190
the same single pane of
glass, to both manage

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:05.180
your SaaS applications and
your on-prem applications.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:08.568
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:15.610
VIDYA NAGARAJAN:
Thanks, Shivani.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:17.240
Now, let's talk about devices.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.930
So we spoke about people,
we spoke about applications,

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:21.830
and now let's talk
about devices.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.460
So Cloud Identity
helps organizations

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.470
to enforce policies consistently
across multiple endpoints,

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:29.542
whether it's iOS,
Android, or Chrome.

00:13:29.542 --> 00:13:31.000
We're happy to
announce that we now

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:36.700
have more than 55 million
30-day devices under management.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:40.680
So admins can enforce
screen locks or passcodes

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.060
to secure devices.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.910
They can wipe corporate data
on devices, view and search

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:47.303
for devices, export details.

00:13:47.303 --> 00:13:49.470
All of this can be done
with basic device management

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:53.190
functionality without the need
of an installed agent, which

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.260
is a very powerful
functionality to secure

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:58.410
your devices while keeping
your users productive.

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:01.350
Admins can whitelist
apps, they can separate

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:04.410
your work and personal
data, distribute apps,

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:07.800
and, lastly, admins can
audit devices, set up rules

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:11.170
to manage these
devices at scale.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:15.090
So we spoke about BeyondCorp
at the start of the session.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.670
BeyondCorp allows a
new way of working

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:19.590
in a perimeterless
or an edgeless world,

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.240
where everything is
based on context.

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:23.730
It's based on the user's
identity and the context

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.390
of that originating request.

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:27.370
So the components in
the BeyondCorp model

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:28.650
are user trust--

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:30.060
you have device trust.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.940
You have the context.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:36.420
So you have a rules engine which
actually then processes access

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.280
to these resources
based on the context--

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.220
what is the IP address,
what is the location, what

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:44.250
is the time, session, so on.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.140
And all of these
components are actually

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:50.550
powered by different assets
in the Google Cloud stack.

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.560
And the assets include
Cloud Identity,

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:54.780
there is something called
endpoint verification,

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:57.935
the Google front end,
access context manager,

00:14:57.935 --> 00:15:00.060
and then you have the
different enforcement points,

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:01.860
like whether it's Google IAP.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:05.370
There is IAM, Cloud Identity,
VPC Service Controls,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:06.600
and so on.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.390
We're happy to announce
that context-aware access is

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:12.130
generally available
for Cloud IAP.

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:14.970
And it's now in open
beta for G Suite,

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:18.630
so please go try it out
and see this in action.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.320
Shivani.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.120
SHIVANI SINHA: Thank you, Vidya.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:26.130
Now, I'm going to demo
context-aware access.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:28.940
So let's say your company has
a lot of confidential files

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:31.250
stored in Google Drive.

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:33.110
And now, your IT Admin
wants to make sure

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:35.840
that employees can only
access Google Drive from corp

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:36.590
network--

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:39.590
so only when you're
in your workplace.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.702
And your IT admin
doesn't care about Gmail.

00:15:41.702 --> 00:15:43.160
They always want
you to check Gmail

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:44.900
so you're up-to-date
with your emails,

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.525
and so they don't
mind if you can check

00:15:46.525 --> 00:15:47.720
your email outside of work

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.560
So let's see this in action.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:52.040
How does your IT admin set
up such a security policy?

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.380
Your IT admin can set
up the security policy

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:00.560
from the context-aware access
page of the Admin console.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.630
Now, I have already set
this policy up for you,

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.180
so I'm going to show
you how this was set up.

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:07.040
If I click on the
Access level, I

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:11.020
can see a policy
called corp IP address.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:13.410
Now, if I click on
Assign Access Levels,

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:17.130
I can see to whom this
access level was assigned.

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.590
So when I click
on this section, I

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:21.660
can see that it
was assigned to me.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.940
Now, I'm present in the
legal organization unit.

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:27.570
So when I click on the
legal organization unit,

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:30.690
I can scroll down and see that
the corp IP address access

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.210
level was assigned
to Drive and Docs.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.610
Now, this means that I
won't be able to access

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.690
Drive and Docs outside of work.

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:40.920
However, you can also see that
it's not assigned to Gmail.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.690
So I should be able to
access Gmail outside of work.

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:45.510
So let's see this in action.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:46.320
Well, where am I?

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:47.070
I'm in a hotel.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:48.880
I'm clearly not in
a Google building,

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:50.430
so I should be able
to access Gmail,

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:52.920
but I shouldn't be
able to access Drive.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.870
So if I try to access Gmail.

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:57.790
Yay, I can access Gmail.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:00.340
And if I try to access
Drive, I shouldn't

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:03.870
be able to access Drive.

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:06.452
There you see, I
don't have access.

00:17:06.452 --> 00:17:08.410
Now, let me walk you
through how you can set up

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.450
more such security policies.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.300
You can set up a security
policy by going here, and then

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.550
creating a new access
level, and then assigning

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:18.339
that access level in
the second section

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:22.220
to a specific organization
unit, your entire domain,

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:24.440
and to a particular application.

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:27.040
So let's go ahead and
create a new access level.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:29.350
Let's say I'm the IT admin
of your organization,

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:31.660
and I don't want you
to access Google Drive

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:33.560
from outside your
corporate device.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:35.480
So if you're using
your personal laptop,

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:37.563
I don't want you to be
able to access Google Drive

00:17:37.563 --> 00:17:38.710
from your personal laptop.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.800
The way I can do that is by
creating a new access level.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:43.540
Let's give it a name--

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:48.250
Corporate device only.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:51.970
When I click on Continue, I
can now set up some conditions.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:56.360
When I click on Attribute, I
want to select Device policy,

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.780
because the is
related to devices.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:00.730
I can click on Yes
for whether the device

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:03.780
should have a password
to add more enforcements.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:05.710
I can add other
restrictions too,

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:09.490
such as I can enforce
a minimum macOS version

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:12.800
or a minimum Chrome OS version.

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:17.820
Now, I need to select Required
under corporate owned device.

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.280
I can add other conditions and
they'll all get compounded.

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:22.140
But for now, I'll
keep it simple,

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:24.000
and I'll click on
Create Access Level

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.380
to create this access level.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.800
Now, I can see that in
addition to corp IP address

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:31.200
and the other access
levels that are here,

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:34.440
I have the Corporate device
only access level created.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.080
I can assign this newly
created access level

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:40.080
to whichever application
that I want to assign it to.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.690
Let's assign it to me again--
because other people are

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:44.820
probably demoing with
the same customer,

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.470
so I don't want to break them--

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:50.610
and assign it to Drive and Docs.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.750
I can compound
multiple access levels.

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:55.120
So I already have the
corp IP address access

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:56.360
level assigned to me.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.650
I can also click on Corporate
device only and hit Save.

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:01.930
So that's how you
create new access levels

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:05.020
and assign these access levels
to whichever organization unit

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:06.130
you want to.

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:08.440
If you want to assign it
to your entire domain,

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.920
you can click on
the entire domain.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:14.440
Now, I'm going to hand it
off to Colin from Essence

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:17.533
to talk about his experiences
using Cloud Identity.

00:19:17.533 --> 00:19:18.700
COLIN MCCARTHY: There we go.

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:19.840
Thank you.

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:21.090
I knew there'd be some glitch.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:22.465
And the other
glitch you can see,

00:19:22.465 --> 00:19:25.690
we've got the status bar on the
screen, which we can't work out

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:27.170
why it's there.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:28.168
But I'm from Essence.

00:19:28.168 --> 00:19:28.960
I'm Colin McCarthy.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.080
I'm the VP for global IT.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:33.730
I've been with the company
for 10 years, nine years,

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:38.320
started in 2010 when it was a
lot simpler time, where we just

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:41.260
had one office and
60 users, and one

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:44.170
app that we would log
into with single sign-on

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:46.480
with SAML-based application.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:49.990
But I wanted to talk to you
about our growth, our journey

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:52.570
using G Suite,
and how we've been

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:58.472
using Cloud Identity and the
context-aware access policy

00:19:58.472 --> 00:20:00.430
that we've been testing
for the last few months

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:05.360
and deployed in North America
and also recently in London.

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:10.360
So Essence, of course, has been
a longtime user of Google Apps.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:13.990
We started with Google
Apps Business in 2009.

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:15.850
And being very much a
cloud-based company,

00:20:15.850 --> 00:20:17.970
that has allowed us to
have a lot of growth,

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:21.590
where in the last nine years
we've grown to 60 offices--

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:26.330
sorry, 19 offices and 1,800
users around the world.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.140
We've also deployed a lot of
the Cloud Identity security

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:32.560
products that have been released
over the last few years--

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:39.840
MFA in 2013, and also Mobile
Device Management in 2014.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:44.380
A show of hands, how many people
have got MFA and Mobile Device

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:47.260
Management on their
Android and iOS devices?

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:49.180
I should see every hand go up.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:51.160
If somebody is hand
hasn't gone up,

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:53.140
I'm sure you're a
little bit embarrassed.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:56.800
If you are using G Suite
and Cloud Identity,

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.410
you certainly should
have MFA and also

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:02.020
Mobile Device
Management deployed

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:04.720
across your enterprise.

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:07.507
There are, obviously,
a number of challenges

00:21:07.507 --> 00:21:08.590
that we have with G Suite.

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:09.860
G Suite is brilliant.

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:13.287
You can access it in any
location, on any device.

00:21:13.287 --> 00:21:15.370
And that's one of the
challenges from accessing it

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:17.800
from any location in any device.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:19.530
Over the last few
years, I've discovered

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:23.680
and seen a lot more stringent
security requirements coming

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:30.410
down from vendors and
clients, where in 2011, 2012,

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:32.410
maybe if you had a new
client they would ask you

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.270
40 security questions.

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.400
Last year, the
security audits that we

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:39.280
would get from clients, and
vendors, and other suppliers

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:43.000
would have 200 to 400
different security questions.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:46.660
So everybody has very much
a security-focused mindset,

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:49.330
and thankfully
with Cloud Identity

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:51.430
and context-aware
access, you now

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:55.030
have the ability
to have confidence

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.760
that you're able to enforce
those corporate policies

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:02.270
across any device that's
accessing your data.

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:05.260
One of the barriers for
deploying any new security

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:08.620
feature is, of course, making
it seamless for the end user.

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:11.890
Because the last thing you
want to do is deploy security

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:15.100
but then inhibit
and make working

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:18.400
with your corporate technology
more of a hindrance to your end

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:19.150
users.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.100
Because if they don't
like that technology

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:25.390
or if it requires too much
of a disruption on the way

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:27.820
that they work, they're
naturally going to bypass that,

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:30.520
and they'll find other ways
of working, which is obviously

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:33.790
how shadow IT has become
such a big corporate problem.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:36.320
So with deploying any
new security feature,

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.680
it's all about
making it seamless

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:41.090
as possible for the end user.

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:43.510
And one of the good things
with context-aware access,

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:45.880
as it was shown, if it's
a corporate-owned machine,

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:49.798
you can upload all the
service or the serial numbers,

00:22:49.798 --> 00:22:51.340
and take ownership
of those machines,

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:53.440
and apply the
policies so that you

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:55.630
know that your data is
secure, but it doesn't really

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:58.700
impact the end user whatsoever.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:02.320
So the challenges that we have
solved with Cloud Identity--

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:05.630
with the BambooHR and
the HRIS integration,

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:07.900
we're going to very much
be able to streamline

00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:12.190
our onboarding process and
also the off-boarding process,

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.890
making sure that accounts are
created at the right time,

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:17.570
people have the right access
to the right resources.

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:20.055
But also, when a
staff member leaves,

00:23:20.055 --> 00:23:22.180
you can make sure that our
account is automatically

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:24.100
deprovisioned.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:25.960
With Mobile Device
Management, we've

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:31.990
always been able to put
in security restrictions

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:36.140
or security policies on
Android and iOS devices.

00:23:36.140 --> 00:23:39.520
Now, we can do those same
policies on Windows and Macs.

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:43.030
And also, with the improvement
in the account security,

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:47.740
we're able to disable
phone-based SMS and phone call

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:50.860
verification, and then
go to the security

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:56.360
key type concept of managing
the MFA for our users.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:59.170
So some of the scenarios
that we've been able to solve

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:02.410
or could potentially solve
at Essence with context-aware

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:03.250
access--

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:06.100
and I'm sure this will be the
same for many organizations

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:06.960
here--

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:10.630
is if you do have temporary
workers in your organization,

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:13.660
you can specify that they
can only access applications

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:17.050
from corporate machines
inside your corporate office.

00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:20.080
You could also make sure that
your full-time employees can

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:24.760
only access data from devices
that comply with your security

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:26.650
policy, which,
obviously, is going

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:29.590
to be an encrypted hard
disk and a password,

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:33.580
and as we shown, potentially,
a minimal level of OS.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:37.150
It's very likely that
we will at some point

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:39.303
disallow the use of Windows 7.

00:24:39.303 --> 00:24:41.470
I only have one user in New
York at the moment who's

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:43.630
still using Windows 7 at home.

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:47.590
I've given her notice that
she needs to upgrade and move

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:48.840
to Windows 10.

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:51.970
It's the same for any
of the macOS versions

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:55.040
that people might be
running with that belief

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:56.920
that, oh, macOS
is secure, I don't

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:59.590
need to update my OS from 2014.

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:05.620
So you do have the ability to
do those OS-level restrictions.

00:25:05.620 --> 00:25:08.760
The other thing you
can do is you can also,

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:10.260
as the product
develops, we're going

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:12.620
to be able to do
time-based restrictions.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:16.150
So you can make sure
that your employees,

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:17.890
dependent on their
role, are only

00:25:17.890 --> 00:25:21.430
able to access applications
through business hours,

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:26.560
or so they all give you
alerts or do restrictions

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:29.980
on accessing data from
high-risk locations,

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:33.527
so that you know that if your
New York employees aren't going

00:25:33.527 --> 00:25:35.110
to be traveling to
China, then there's

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.450
no reason why their
account should be logging

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:41.700
in on China or Slovakia or any
other country around the world.

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:44.380
Also, you can specify
that employees that

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:46.390
are leaving your
company, maybe you,

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:48.130
as part of the
off-boarding process,

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:52.150
maybe you say that they can only
access their corporate network

00:25:52.150 --> 00:25:54.890
from inside the
corporate office.

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:57.940
It's just a good security
way that with this you

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:02.900
can manage the onboarding
and the off-boarding process.

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:05.440
So how did we deploy it?

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:08.050
This was, for us
and my colleagues,

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:10.420
was probably the
smoothest, largest

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:15.370
deployment that we have done
to date, over all of our years.

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:17.410
Everybody, I'm assuming,
has the ability

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:20.540
to push out an application to
their corporate end devices,

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:23.870
whether you're using
Jamf or Microsoft Intune.

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:27.030
So we pushed out the native
helper that's required.

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:30.370
I enforced foreseeing the
Chrome extension to be installed

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.590
for everybody's profile.

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:36.950
But what we did before we
did all of those, I guess,

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.850
the technical side of it,
we spent a lot of time

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:42.220
on the human side of it
and made sure that we

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:44.260
had executive buy-in.

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:47.620
The COO was on-board
with the changes

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:49.240
that we were going to make.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.257
We alerted all of
the regional office

00:26:51.257 --> 00:26:52.840
leads that we were
going to be pushing

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:56.060
this out to their staff.

00:26:56.060 --> 00:27:00.150
And then we also did a
series of emails or notices

00:27:00.150 --> 00:27:04.280
on our internal system, telling
users what was going to happen.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:07.478
And we also educated our
support staff and told them,

00:27:07.478 --> 00:27:09.520
this is the change that
we're going to be making,

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.160
these are probably
the issues that you're

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:14.090
going to come across.

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:18.130
And if you put out a good
documentation to your end users

00:27:18.130 --> 00:27:22.180
about a change, you can
probably expect that 10% to 20%

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:25.070
of those users aren't going to
read or action those documents.

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:27.630
So if you have 500
users, your help desk

00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:31.350
can expect to have between
50 and 100 users having

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:33.577
problems on any particular day.

00:27:33.577 --> 00:27:35.410
And that's what my poor
colleagues in London

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:38.820
had on Monday, when we
enforced context-aware access.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:42.610
We spend a lot of time
working with our end users.

00:27:42.610 --> 00:27:44.710
I did a small trial.

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:48.280
I tested it on my test
account, got very familiar

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:51.910
with the application
and the policies that

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:53.160
could be enforced.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:56.540
Then did it to four people,
did it to 20 people,

00:27:56.540 --> 00:27:58.530
and then pushed it
out of 400 people.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:01.270
And probably in the space of
about 10 days, did all of that,

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:03.340
and really honed
the communication

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:05.170
and created some
animated GIFs so they

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:07.710
could see the whole process.

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:11.830
And that was our
process through that.

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:13.227
Thank you very much.

00:28:13.227 --> 00:28:16.706
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:21.580
VIDYA NAGARAJAN:
Thank you, Colin.

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:22.080
OK.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:25.440
So now, let's put
this all together

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:28.170
in terms of defining
what a typical customer

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:30.810
journey could look like.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:34.830
So Acme Corp is a company
which has many of its IT Assets

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:36.060
on premise.

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:39.330
Many of their systems, their
devices, their print servers,

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.520
their VPN servers,
et cetera, are

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:43.680
integrated into Active
Directory for authentication

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:45.600
or for other identity needs.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:48.930
Acme Corp's CIO has decided that
they want to move to the cloud

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:51.840
so that they can embrace
this BeyondCorp philosophy,

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:54.360
and they can make their
users and employees

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:57.660
way more productive, and work
securely so that they can work

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:59.760
from anywhere, from any device.

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:05.100
And also, it helps increase Acme
Corp's return on investment.

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:08.010
So first, to integrate
what with Cloud Apps,

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:11.820
Acme Corp Decides to replace
their on-premise SSO solution

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:13.040
with Cloud Identity.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.540
So they use the tool called the
Google Cloud Directory Sync,

00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:19.150
and that tool essentially syncs
information, users, groups,

00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:21.390
and other metadata information
from Active Directory

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:24.150
into Google's Cloud Directory.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:26.760
They also replace their
productivity suite

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.440
with G Suite.

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:31.920
Now, that they have successfully
integrated all their cloud apps

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:34.380
with Cloud Identity,
they start integrating

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:38.460
their legacy on-premise
LDAP-based applications,

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:41.310
using Cloud Identity's
Secure LDAP functionality

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:44.520
that we visited earlier.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:47.670
They can also then
integrate their VPN servers

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.400
as part of their IT
infrastructure, Mac or Linux

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:52.500
devices with Secure
LDAP, and potentially

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:54.210
also manage all their
other Windows-type

00:29:54.210 --> 00:29:58.180
of devices with Cloud
Identity in the future.

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:00.713
So now, you've heard quite
a bit this afternoon,

00:30:00.713 --> 00:30:02.880
and you can see there's a
ton of great functionality

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:04.410
that is in the product.

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:06.990
And the good news is,
all of this functionality

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:10.110
is available for
one single license.

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:12.900
And many of you in this room
might be existing G Suite

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.690
customers, or you might be
using Google Cloud Platform,

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:17.820
and you might wonder,
how does licensing

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:20.520
work in such a situation?

00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:23.760
So the good news is, Cloud
Identity's free functionality

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:29.370
is available or is built into G
Suite Basic and Business SKUs.

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:32.910
If you are a G Suite
Enterprise user,

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.260
the Cloud Identity
standalone product,

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:37.710
which we call as Cloud
Identity Premium,

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:39.870
is built into that product.

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:42.180
So as a customer, if you
want to use functionality

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:45.600
like context-aware access,
which Shivani demoed earlier,

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:49.980
you have the option of buying or
upgrading to G Suite Enterprise

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:53.310
or you could buy the standalone
Cloud Identity product

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:57.450
and use that alongside
your existing G Suite Basic

00:30:57.450 --> 00:31:00.490
and Business subscriptions.

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:03.010
If you are using Google
Cloud Platform today,

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:05.410
you can sign up for
the free Cloud Identity

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:10.070
account as part of your regular
cloud onboarding experience.

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:12.980
However, if you require Premium
identity functionality--

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:16.600
for example, if you want session
management controls so that you

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:19.840
can actually control session
lengths for your different

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:23.053
accessing your cloud
admin console--

00:31:23.053 --> 00:31:24.970
you would then need the
Cloud Identity Premium

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:30.100
product which you can
then add-on as an upgrade.

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:34.030
So in a nutshell, Cloud
Identity is a unified platform

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:37.570
to manage your people,
devices, and applications.

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:41.860
It enables the BeyondCorp
vision and allows your users

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.350
to be truly productive
but extremely secure,

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:46.660
with features like
context-aware access.

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:48.600
It increases productivity
with single sign-on

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:50.800
to thousands of applications,
both by supporting

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:53.320
federated standards
like SAML and Open IDC,

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:57.250
as well as password-based
SSO for those applications

00:31:57.250 --> 00:32:00.130
that don't support the
federated standards.

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:02.320
It provides account
security with MFA,

00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:04.270
as well as security keys--

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:06.580
security keys from outside
or the Titan security key

00:32:06.580 --> 00:32:07.600
from Google.

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:11.380
And it very much
enables hybrid identity

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:14.440
and meets organizations where
they are in your current cloud

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:15.320
journey.

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:18.670
So this is either with the
help of tools like Secure LDAP

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.400
or directory sync tools like
the Google Cloud Directory Sync,

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.890
which syncs information
from your on-premise systems

00:32:23.890 --> 00:32:25.660
to Google's Cloud Directory.

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.190
And again, all of
this is available

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:31.090
for a single subscription,
and you can sign up today

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:32.090
for a free trial.

00:32:32.090 --> 00:32:36.320
So please do sign-up, and
let us know how it works out.

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:39.750
You can go to
cloud.google.com/identity

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:43.600
and check the product out for
yourself for a 14-day trial,

00:32:43.600 --> 00:32:45.860
and give us your feedback.

00:32:45.860 --> 00:32:46.360
Thank you.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:49.410
